    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0624d1b5_0db6aad5",
        "filename": "android/app/src/com/android/bluetooth/le_scan/ScanManager.java",
        "patchSetId": 25
      },
      "lineNbr": 2032,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962300
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-15T08:58:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Besides the OPPORTUNISTIC case, the `.started` usage is incorrect here because the client we\u0027re currently handling would have started \u003d\u003d false. See `handleStartScan`, `.started` is set after `startRegularScan`.\n\nReading into the `startRegularScan` and `stopRegularScan` implementation, I think the below simply works: Both `startRegularScan` and `stopRegularScan` treat the non-OPPORTUNISTIC scans in `mRegularScanClients` as started, regardless of the `.started` value.\n\nAlso note the null filters case \u0026\u0026 the out-of-filter-index case. I think `mClientFilterIndexMap` handles it better because all and only the all-pass clients would be in the map.\n\n```suggestion\n                    !mRegularScanClients.stream().anyMatch(c -\u003e {\n                        if (c.settings !\u003d null \u0026\u0026 c.settings.getScanMode() \u003d\u003d ScanSettings.SCAN_MODE_OPPORTUNISTIC) {\n                            return false;\n                        }\n                        return !this.mClientFilterIndexMap.containsKey(c.scannerId)\n                    });\n```",
